WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:03.603
Now, you're a director.
I mean, this one that I saw--

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.205
I mean, if you do one,
are you still considered--

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.607
-BILL MAHER: Yeah.
-(CHUCKLES)

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.042
(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.810
There may...
may not be repeat business,

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:12.845
-but certainly--
-RAY ROMANO: Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:15.381
No, I think there will be.
I mean, I enjoyed it a lot

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.918
and it's funny, you know,
it's about this Italian family,

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:20.853
but, like, in the sitcom,
you didn't play up

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:22.188
the Italian part all that much.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.989
-This family--
-RAY: Yeah.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:26.125
They're--
I mean, dinner at noon,

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.160
-what the fuck is that?
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:30.863
-(CHUCKLES)
-Is that your real--

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.999
I think they started
intermittent fasting,

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.833
-the Italians, you know 'cause--
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:36.736
What is that
dinner at noon thing

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.137
and is that
what your family was,

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.039
-your real family?
-Well, here's the thing.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:44.410
The family that I wrote
the movie about,

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:47.113
is the one I married into.
I married into that family.

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:48.514
I'm Italian,
I grew up in New York.

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:50.116
-BILL: I see.
-My parents are from this--

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.818
Were born here though,
but my wife's parents

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:55.522
came right over on the boat,
and didn't speak English.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.890
And that's the universe
I wanted to write about.

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:01.427
And yes, on Sunday,
dinner was around noon.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.998
Yes, that's true. It's a--
Listen it sounds like a cliché

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:06.633
and some of the characters
we thought,

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.502
are we stereotyping too much,
but it was--

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.005
When I started dating my wife,

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.640
I know this is gonna sound
like a stereotype.

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:17.977
Like, week two,
I called her house up

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:20.646
and I said,
"Is Anna home? It's Ray."

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:22.682
And her mother spoke
broken English

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:24.884
and she said,
"No, you know, no home."

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.919
And I said, "You just tell her
that Ray called."

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.587
And she goes,
"Okay, you hungry?"

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:30.590
-On the phone.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:32.958
-(CHUCKLES)
-RAY: I know, it sounds...

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:36.462
it sounds like it,
but on the phone

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.364
she wanted to know
if I was hungry.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.067
So, that's what-- I wrote...
I wrote about what I knew

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.701
and I've known that for 30--

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.737
-I've been married 35 years, so.
-(AUDIENCE APPLAUDING)

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:49.242
-(CHUCKLES)
-(CHUCKLES)

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.644
So, that's a mock clap.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.146
No, no. It's just odd to me
that two people

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.615
who are, like,
around the same age, 40.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:59.218
-RAY: Yeah.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.454
-Who? You and me?
-Yeah, of course.

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.724
Who, like, had very similar
East Coast upbringings.

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:06.392
I mean, I... of course,

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.561
at this neighborhood,
a lot of Italian people,

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:10.163
and I didn't really see them
that much

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.632
in the neighborhood at school.
I was a delivery boy

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:15.601
for the local liquor
and drug store,

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.069
there's like
three stores in town.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.237
-RAY: Right.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.805
And I delivered for two of them
and I would--

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.641
So, I would see Italians
in their natural habitat--

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:26.746
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)
-(CHUCKLES)

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:30.783
(CHUCKLES)
My first girlfriend was Italian.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.752
-RAY: Yeah, you grew up in...
-BILL: New Jersey.

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.520
-Jersey, right? Yeah.
-BILL: Bergen County,
New Jersey.

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:35.921
-Yeah.
-RAY: Yeah.

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.590
No, I mean, look. I used to--

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:39.158
BILL: But how did we become
so different

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.094
with such similar age
and background--

00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:44.830
Well, we're the same
in some ways, right?

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.399
-Comic.
-We still like to perform,

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:47.467
-and stand up.
-Yes.

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:48.568
You're smarter,
you're much smarter.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.202
-No. No.
-RAY: No, you are.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.138
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)
-RAY: (CHUCKLES) I know.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:54.606
Well, we'll see who richer
and then we'll find out
who's smarter.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.545
(AUDIENCE CHEERING, APPLAUDING)

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:02.114
I think I win that,
I'm not sure.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:06.452
I know, I think you probably do.
I'm pretty sure you do.

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.187
But neither one of us
are complaining.

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.122
-I don't think
-BILL: No, no, no.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:13.926
I think it's a testament to you
that a guy like me

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.962
watches your show every week
because you--

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.098
Of course, you have
the intelligent audience,

00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:20.299
but you cross over.

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:22.602
-You have the dumb audience too.
-(AUDIENCE LAUGHING)

